## Solution

**Setup.** Let the coordinates be $(x^1, x^2) = (x, y)$. The metric components are
$$g_{11} = 1,\quad g_{12} = g_{21} = 0,\quad g_{22} = x^2 = (x^1)^2,$$
and the vector field components are $X^1 = 1,\; X^2 = 0$ (i.e. $X = \partial/\partial x$).

**Formula.** The Lie derivative of a $(0,2)$-tensor is
$$(\mathcal{L}_X g)_{ab} = X^c\,\partial_c g_{ab} + g_{cb}\,\partial_a X^c + g_{ac}\,\partial_b X^c.$$

**Key observation.** The components $X^1 = 1$ and $X^2 = 0$ are constants, so
$$\partial_a X^c = 0 \quad\text{for all } a, c.$$
The last two terms vanish, leaving
$$(\mathcal{L}_X g)_{ab} = X^c\,\partial_c g_{ab} = \partial_1 g_{ab}\quad(\text{since } X^1=1,\, X^2=0).$$

**Component-wise computation.**

- $g_{11} = 1 \;\Rightarrow\; \partial_1 g_{11} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; (\mathcal{L}_X g)_{11} = 0.$
- $g_{12} = g_{21} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \partial_1 g_{12} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; (\mathcal{L}_X g)_{12} = (\mathcal{L}_X g)_{21} = 0.$
- $g_{22} = (x^1)^2 \;\Rightarrow\; \partial_1 g_{22} = 2x^1 = 2x \;\Rightarrow\; (\mathcal{L}_X g)_{22} = 2x.$

**Result.**
$$\boxed{(\mathcal{L}_X g)_{ab} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 2x \end{pmatrix}}$$

**Sanity check.** The metric $\mathrm{d}s^2 = \mathrm{d}x^2 + x^2\,\mathrm{d}y^2$ is the Euclidean metric in polar coordinates ($x \leftrightarrow r$, $y \leftrightarrow \theta$). The vector field $X = \partial_x = \partial_r$ is the radial unit vector. The Lie derivative $\mathcal{L}_X g$ measures the rate of change of the metric under radial flow; only the angular component $g_{\theta\theta} = r^2$ changes, at rate $2r = 2x$, exactly as computed. ∎

